rlm-gar-rm [-n|--dry-run]

Delete Artifact Registry <repo>/<package> entries picked in an fzf
picker. TAB multi-selects; every selected package is confirmed
individually before anything is destroyed.

The package list and its cache are shared with gar-open — both
commands read and refresh the same files under ~/.cache/gar-open/, so
a refresh in either benefits the other. Selecting --- REFRESH CACHE ---
opens a secondary picker where you TAB-multi-select which projects to
re-fetch; entries for un-picked projects are preserved.

ORDERING

Packages are listed least-recently-pushed first, and the left column
shows how long it has been since the last image push (today, 6d, 351d,
1y19d). The stalest packages — the ones most likely to be deletable —
therefore sit at the top.

Unlike gar-open, gar-rm ignores the shared MRU history: a package you
picked recently is the opposite of stale, so letting recency float
entries to the top would work against the question this command exists
to answer.

The timestamp is the updateTime of the package's newest version, read
from the versions API during a cache refresh. It is deliberately not
the package-level updateTime that the package listing returns for
free: that field also moves on metadata writes and on registry-side
housekeeping, so a long-dead package can report a recent date. One
package here showed "5d" while its newest image dated to 2025-07-24,
a year out — and the error runs in the dangerous direction, hiding a
stale package at the bottom of the list instead of surfacing it.

Packages cached before ages were recorded show "unknown" and sort
first, on the principle that an entry whose age cannot be determined
deserves a look rather than a pass. Refresh the cache to fill them in.

The preview pane is deletion-oriented rather than browse-oriented
(that is gar-open's job): it reports the version count, total size on
disk, newest and oldest version timestamps, tag count, package
create/update time and annotations, the number of 1000-version delete
batches required, and a sample of the newest tagged versions. If the
version count cannot be determined — expired credentials, missing IAM
permission — the preview says so loudly instead of rendering an empty
package, because "no versions" reads as "safe to delete".

DELETION

A single `gcloud artifacts packages delete` removes the package and
every version and tag in it, server-side, waiting for the operation to
complete. The exact command is printed before the confirmation.

That call does not scale indefinitely. Measured on a 1159-version
package: it ran about three minutes, deleted 999 versions, then failed
with "Internal error encountered", leaving 160 versions and the package
in place. Treat roughly 1000 versions as the practical ceiling per
call; packages above that are flagged before you confirm.

On a partial delete, gar-rm reports how many versions were removed and
how many remain, then asks whether to delete the rest. It does not
retry on its own — each retry is another destructive call, so it is
your decision. Answering no leaves the remainder in place and counts
the package as failed. If an attempt deletes nothing at all (a
permissions problem, say), gar-rm says so and stops rather than
prompting for a retry that cannot help.

Version counts are read over the REST API rather than gcloud: they are
taken before and after every attempt, and gcloud costs over a second of
startup per invocation.

Packages successfully deleted are pruned from the shared cache and the
MRU history so the next picker does not offer them.

OPTIONS
  -n, --dry-run
              Show the preview, the version counts, and the exact
              commands for every selected package, but run no deletes
              and ask no confirmations.

  -h, --help  Print usage and exit.

ENVIRONMENT
  GAR_SEARCH_PROJECTS
              Colon-separated list of GCP project IDs to browse.
              Required; the function errors out if not set. Shared
              with gar-open and gar-search.

  GAR_SEARCH_LOCATIONS
              Colon-separated Artifact Registry locations to scan
              (default europe:europe-west1). Repositories are
              listed per location — the API has no "all locations"
              query — so a location missing from this list is
              invisible to the picker.

REQUIRES
  gcloud, fzf, jq, curl, python3.

SEE ALSO
  gar-open    Browse the same package list and open a package in the
              Artifact Registry console.
  gar-search  Non-interactive substring search over the same packages.

NOTE
  OSC 8 hyperlinks in the preview pane require a terminal emulator
  that supports them (Kitty, Ghostty, iTerm2, WezTerm). Inside tmux
  also set: set -ga terminal-features "*:hyperlinks"
